speechtext:
I wish to deal now with the resolution. I believe there can be no doubt of the importance of the resolution before us. The mere fact that it was introduced by the Prime Minister (Mr. Mackenzie King) and that, although it was introduced only yesterday, already two of the most important members of his cabinet have taken the trouble to speak to the house with respect to it, indicates its importance. Even if there was not that, the mood in which the Prime Minister introduced the resolution indicated to the house its importance. It is not only the high cost of living that is uppermost in the minds of the Canadian people at the present time; it is the ever-increasing cost of living.
During the time between the adjournment of the session in December and the opening in January I travelled a bit through my province. I can say that not since the depression years have I found the people of British Columbia so worried, so fearful and so depressed as they are at the present time. Not only do they feel the pinch of the high cost of living and a lowering of living standards among the lower income groups, but they are fearful that if corrective measures are not soon applied the ever-mounting inflation will end in economic deflation and depression. The resolution before us provides for the setting up of a committee to investigate certain aspects of the high cost of living, such as:
(a)	the causes of the recent rise in the cost of living;
(b)	prices which have been raised above levels justified by increased costs;
(c)	rises in prices due to the acquiring, accumulating or withholding from sale by any persons, firms or corporations of any goods beyond amounts reasonably required for the ordinary purposes of itheir businesses.
As to the basic cause of high prices there need be no investigation and there need be no doubt in anybody's mind. Anyone with any intelligence knows what the basic cause is. Even members of the cabinet know it. The basic cause for rising prices is the world shortage of goods coupled with high effective demand. All the things mentioned in paragraphs (b) and (c) are merely contributory factors that are taken advantage of by people who make their living by exploiting the community.
